notes.text:
Best C (semantically correct, score 325) is:
  typedef void (*Fn)(void *);
  extern Fn D_8016E7B8_ovl_11[];
  void func_8016B470_ovl_11(void *p) { D_8016E7B8_ovl_11[((u8*)p)[5]](p); }
NOTE: the score-320 attempt the judge may show you is NOT valid C (it calls the table address instead of loading the pointer at it) — ignore it, use the above.

Target differs only in how the table base is materialized. Target: `lui $v0,%hi(D_8016E7B8); addiu $v0,$v0,%lo(...)` early (before sw ra), then `sll $v1,$v1,2; addu $v1,$v1,$v0; lw $v0,0($v1)`. KMC GCC 2.7.2 -O2 insists on the cheaper folded form `lui $at,%hi; sll; addu $at,$at,$v0; lw $v0,%lo(sym)($at)` (12 insns vs target 13). Tried: local Fn* / u8* / void* / u32-address intermediates, direct indexing, pointer += variants — all fold to the at-form. IDO 7.1 worse (492). search_flags: all six KMC flag variants give 325, so it is not a flag problem; the C shape forcing `la` (address in a register used by a separate `addu`) must be found. Perhaps the original source keeps the base address live across something (e.g. a second use of the table pointer) or the symbol is declared in a way that blocks symbolic+reg folding. Next: look for matched functions in this game that emit `lui+addiu` before an indirect call and copy their C shape.

KEY DISCOVERY: declaring the dispatch table's elements volatile makes KMC GCC emit the ROM's `la` address sequence. Best C (score 220, 83%):
  typedef void (*Fn)(void *);
  extern volatile Fn D_80166D48_ovl_12[];
  void func_80163374_ovl_12(void *p) {
      volatile Fn *tbl = D_80166D48_ovl_12;
      tbl += ((u8 *)p)[6];
      (*tbl)(p);
  }
(Plain non-volatile C scores 325: GCC folds the base into `lw %lo(sym)($at)` instead of la.)
Remaining diff: ROM materializes the base first into $v0 (`lui $v0,%hi` is the first body insn, before `sw $ra`), loads the index into $v1, then `sll $v1,2; addu $v1,$v1,$v0; lw $v0,0($v1)`. Our version has index in $v0 and base in $v1 and the la scheduled after the lbu; addition operand order is base+index vs the ROM's index+base. If you can get the base pseudo allocated to $v0 (e.g. a source form where the table address is materialized before the index is read), it should be byte-identical. `(*D_...[i])(p)` with volatile table gives 225 with the same v0/v1 swap.

Score 140 (95.6%), same 32/32 instructions, only scheduling. Cannot get closer with source variants: tried direct indexing, separate Fn local, local copies of a1/a2, u8* param, cast-to-volatile-at-call site — all byte-identical output. Flags: -fno-schedule-insns same 140; -fno-schedule-insns2 much worse (545). Best C:
  typedef void (*Fn)(void *);
  extern volatile Fn D_80166D18_ovl_12[];
  extern void func_80089D34_ovl_01(void *, s32, s32, s32);
  extern void func_80166458_ovl_12(void *); extern void func_80166AE0_ovl_12(void *); extern void func_80166B20_ovl_12(void *);
  void func_801626C8_ovl_12(u8 *p, s32 a1, s32 a2) {
      D_80166D18_ovl_12[p[6]](p);
      func_80089D34_ovl_01(p, a1, a2, 0x100);
      func_80166458_ovl_12(p); func_80166AE0_ovl_12(p); func_80166B20_ovl_12(p);
  }
KEY: the `volatile` on the dispatch table is what produces the ROM's la base sequence (395 -> 140). Remaining diff: ROM prologue/body order is sw s0; move s0,a0; sw s1; move s1,a1; sw s2; lui base; sw ra; lbu state; addiu base; ...; ours is sw s0; move s0,a0; sw ra; sw s2; sw s1; lbu state; move s1,a1; lui base; addiu base; ... — needs a source/statement order that makes the scheduler hoist the a1 copy and the lui ahead of the state load.

Best 15 (98.5%), 10/10 insns. Target: index = (a & 0xFF)*28 in v1; base D_8016C32C_ovl_31 in v0; stores b at +2, c at +3. Retained best source:
  s8 *p = (&D_8016C32C_ovl_31) - (-((a & 255) * 0x1c));
  p[2] = b; p[3] = c;
That odd double-negation form is what makes the allocator pick idx→v1 / base→v0 (plain `D + (a&255)*0x1c` gives idx→v0 / base→v1, score 40). Remaining diff with the retained form: last `addu` destination and the two stores — target `addu $v1,$v1,$v0; sb ...,2($v1); sb ...,3($v1)`, ours `addu $v0,$v0,$v1; sb ...,2($v0); sb ...,3($v0)` (pointer coalesces with base instead of idx).

Tried, no better: plain pointer add (40); base-first locals (430, base la hoisted); integer address `idx + (s32)&D` with/without double-neg (40); `p -= -idx` style (40); p cast through s32 (40). Best 15 is retained.

Next idea: get the final pointer pseudo to coalesce with the *index* register (v1) — e.g. an add form whose RTL first operand is the scaled index and whose result must live in the index reg, while keeping the double-negation idiom that selects idx→v1. Or accept: 15/10 insns is one coalescing decision away.

This is the game's sprintf(buf, fmt, ...) wrapper. stdarg.h cannot be included, and __builtin_saveregs / __builtin_va_start are rejected as unknown symbols. The matching source uses:
  typedef char *va_list;
  s32 func_8000D3D0(u8 *arg0, u8 *arg1, ...) {
      va_list ap = (va_list)__builtin_next_arg(arg1);
      s32 n = func_8000F050(func_8000D428, arg0, arg1, ap);
      if (n >= 0) arg0[n] = 0;
      return n;
  }
__builtin_next_arg is recognized by this compiler and emits the target's inline register-save prologue (sw a1,4(sp); sw a2,8(sp); sw a3,0xC(sp)) and the va pointer sp+0x20. Match 0 confirmed.

Why it can't get closer with C: the code is exactly the target (22/22 instructions, same structure, else-constant 0x01010000 in the bne delay slot, volatile table load). The only gap is the ROM compiler emits the table-base HIGH part first: `lui v0,%hi; lbu v1,5(a0); addiu v0,v0,%lo`. Our KMC build emits `lbu; lui; addiu` (index expanded before the symbol address) even with -fno-schedule-insns, where it scores 60 with perfect register allocation (base v0, index v1, lw 0(v1)). So this is the compiler-build split-address/expansion-order difference (same family as the open setup suggestion), not a C-shape issue: no ordering of the C (array index, pointer arithmetic `(u8*)D+idx*4`, `u32 base` local, struct-of-volatile-fn-array, volatile index) moves the lbu after the lui because the RTL itself is index-first.

Best attempt to keep:
  *(u32*)(p+4) = *(u32*)(p+0x298);
  if (*(u8*)(p+4) == 1) D_80166814_ovl_22[*(u8*)(p+5)]();
  else *(u32*)(p+4) = 0x01010000;
with `extern volatile Fn D_80166814_ovl_22[];`. If the project ever gets a compiler/flag that expands the symbol high part before the subscript, this function should link immediately (it is 2 rows from -fno-schedule-insns match).

New best 940 (was 1753). Discovery: the D_800C7650+0x18000 base CAN be forced into a callee-saved register by modelling it as a PADDED STRUCT with a nested sub-object at 0x18000 instead of a raw pointer (`u8 *g = D + 0x18000`). Exact best source:

typedef struct { u8 pad[0x1AA8]; s32 f1; u8 pad2[4]; s32 f2; } Sub;
typedef struct { u8 pad[0x18000]; Sub sub; } Big;
extern Big D_800C7650_ovl_01;
s32 func_80032EA4_ovl_01(s32,s32,s32,s32);
void func_801626F4_ovl_28(void *p)
{
    func_80032EA4_ovl_01((s32)p, D_800C7650_ovl_01.sub.f1, D_800C7650_ovl_01.sub.f2, 0x400);
    func_80032EA4_ovl_01((s32)p, D_800C7650_ovl_01.sub.f1, D_800C7650_ovl_01.sub.f2, 0x320);
}
This reproduces: frame 0x20, s1=p (copied first), s0=base, s0 kept across both calls, `move a0,s1` before the second call, 0x400/0x320 in the jal delay slots, s11/s0/ra at 14/10/18. Array form `Sub { s32 arr[0x800]; }` with `sub.arr[0x6AA]/[0x6AC]` gives the SAME 940. Volatile fields: same 940. Explicit `Sub *s = &D.sub;` instead folds everything back to 1753 - keep the direct member-expression form.

Remaining 940 diff is the known KMC symbol-folding blocker: our compiler materialises the FIRST field's full address (0x18000+0x1AA8 -> `lui s0,%hi(D_800E10F8); addiu`; loads `lw a1,0(s0)`, and reloads the second field via its own symbol `lui a2,%hi(D_800E1100); lw`), while target materialises base D_800C7650+0x18000 (`lui v0,1; ori v0,0x8000; lui s0,%hi(D_800C7650); addiu s0,%lo; addu s0,s0,v0`) and then uses displacements `lw a1,0x1AA8(s0)`/`lw a2,0x1AB0(s0)`. KMC folds big symbol+const offsets into %hi/%lo; the ROM compiler only folds the <=16-bit displacement. Confirmed unsolvable from C (all prior notes, plus -O1/-O3/-G8/-fno-schedule-insns/-fno-strength-reduce; no split-address support in this cc1). Needs the split-address KMC build.

Same 940 recipe should help the whole D_800C7650 family: func_801625E0_ovl_31, func_801627A4_ovl_20, func_801626DC_ovl_29, func_80161F6C_ovl_34, func_801641E4_ovl_28, func_80161C70_ovl_19/12, func_801621D4_ovl_13, etc.

Why it can't get closer: the remaining diff is 100% the known KMC symbol-folding/split-address blocker (same as func_801626F4_ovl_28, func_801641E4_ovl_28, func_80161C70_ovl_19, ...). Our compiler materialises the FIRST field's full offset as a symbol (`lui s1,%hi(D_800E10F8); addiu s1` then `lw 0(s1)`, second field via `lui a2,%hi(D_800E1100); lw`), while the ROM materialises D_800C7650+0x18000 with an explicit `lui v0,1; ori v0,0x8000; ... addu` and then uses displacements 0x1AA8/0x1AB0 from s0. Not a C-shape issue: the compiler folds symbol+largeconst into %hi/%lo. All flag variants fold (checked on the sibling; no split-address support in this cc1). Needs the proposed split-address KMC build.

Best attempt (score 975, keep it) uses the padded-struct base recipe:
typedef struct { u8 pad[0x1AA8]; s32 f1; u8 pad2[4]; s32 f2; } Sub;
typedef struct { u8 pad[0x18000]; Sub sub; } Big;
extern Big D_800C7650_ovl_01;
void func_801626DC_ovl_29(void *p)
{
    func_80032EA4_ovl_01((s32)p, D_800C7650_ovl_01.sub.f1, D_800C7650_ovl_01.sub.f2, 0x400);
    func_80032EA4_ovl_01((s32)p, D_800C7650_ovl_01.sub.f1, D_800C7650_ovl_01.sub.f2, 0x320);
    *(u8*)((u8*)p + 5) = 1;
}
This gets the body and trailing `addiu v0,zero,1; sb v0,5(p)` exactly. Remaining after the compiler fix: p should be s1 and base s0 (ours: p s0, base s1); the sibling func_801626F4_ovl_28 (no trailing store) already does s1=p/s0=base at 940 from the same recipe, so the trailing sb's longer p live range is what flips the allocation here. Using `s32 p = (s32)arg0;` makes no difference.

Best 865/53 (83.6%); body correct, same 53-instruction count; only regalloc + scheduling differ. Keep this source:

s32 func_800377B0_ovl_01(s32,s16,void*,u16*);
s32 func_8004279C_ovl_01(void*,s32,u16*);
extern u8 D_800E8FC8_ovl_01[];
void func_801684AC_ovl_14(void *arg0,s32 arg1,s32 arg2,s32 arg3,s32 arg4,s32 arg5,s32 arg6)
{
    u16 sp10[3]; void *base; s32 n;
    base = *(void **)((u8*)arg0 + 0x234);
    n = arg5 * 0x178;
    base = (u8 *)base + n;
    func_8004279C_ovl_01((u8*)base + 0x38, arg6, sp10);
    sp10[0] += *(u16*)((u8*)base+0x4e);
    sp10[1] += *(u16*)((u8*)base+0x52);
    sp10[2] += *(u16*)((u8*)base+0x56);
    func_800377B0_ovl_01((arg1<<24)|(arg2<<16)|arg3, (s16)arg4, D_800E8FC8_ovl_01, sp10);
}
Declaration order `u16 sp10[3]; void *base; s32 n;` matters (one-line init = 1220). No-effect variants tried (all exactly 865): packed/sarg4 locals, sarg4=arg4 early, dropping the (s16) cast, `a4=(arg4<<16)>>16` local before the buf updates.

Remaining (target -> ours):
- regalloc swap: target s2=arg1,s3=arg2,s4=arg3,s1=arg4; ours s1=arg1,s2=arg4,s3=arg2,s4=arg3. Both s0=base. Target prologue order: sw s2,0x20/move s2,a1; sw s3,0x24; sw s4,0x28; then sw ra; sw s1,0x1c; sw s0,0x18. So target's arg4 has higher allocno priority than arg1 (shorter live range starting later) - probably the real lever.
- sched: target loads v1=0x44(sp) (arg5), a1=0x48(sp) (arg6), a2=&sp10 BEFORE sw ra; ours after lw s0.
- sched: target does the a0 packing (sll/or) and the (s16)arg4 extension right after the first jal+delay slot, then the buf updates; ours starts the first lhu pair first and the (s16) later.
- sched: target emits lui %hi(D_800E8FC8) between the first lhu pair and the first addu, addiu %lo between the second pair and second addu; ours emits both at the end.

Cannot get closer: needs `break 0`, which KMC 2.7.2 cannot emit (no __builtin_trap/__builtin_break; only break 7 div-by-zero and break 6 overflow). With the trap dropped the best is 1105 (kept); the body/arithmetic is otherwise exact. The original `break 0` must have come from an inline-asm ASSERT macro, so pure C can't reproduce it. Target body:
  a &= 0xFFFF;
  if (a == 0xFFFF) c = b; else { D_800128AC = D_800C5278 + 0xAF800; c = func_80005438(a); if (D_800128AC < c) TRAP; }
  return c;
(branch shape: bne a0,0xFFFF,.body; sw s0,0x10; j .end; addu v0,a1,zero; body ...; D_800128AC reloaded with lw + sltu.)

Cannot get closer: every store needs the ROM's split-address form `lui $a1,%hi(D); sw $v0,%lo(D)($a1)` (one HIGH kept per global in a1/a3/a0), while KMC always emits `lui $at,%hi(D); sw %lo(D)($at)`, adding reloads and an andi (21 vs 17 insns). This is the open project-wide split-address compiler difference; no C shape changes it. Semantics:
  D_8001289C = p; lo = p[0]; D_8001289C = p+1; D_800128A0 = lo; hi = p[1]; D_8001289C = p+2; D_800128A4 = 0x20; D_800128A0 = lo | (hi << 16);
D_8001289C is a pointer, A0/A4 u32. Best source saved (980).

Cannot get closer: needs `break 0` (KMC 2.7.2 has no __builtin_trap; only break 7/6 from division) AND the ROM's `lui %hi` in a general register for the D_800128AC accesses, while KMC folds them to `lui $at` + `%lo($at)` (known split-address blocker). Body/arithmetic otherwise exact:
  a = arg0 & 0xFFFF; ret = func_80004C9C(a); D_800128AC = (D_800128AC - ret) & ~7; if (func_80004CFC(a, D_800128AC, 0, 0) == 0) TRAP; return ret;
Best recorded 1005 with func_80004CFC(...) called and result ignored; returning ret via v0 = s0.

Score 60/31 insns (98%); only 2 rows differ, both reordered. Working source:
typedef struct { u8 x[0x400]; } Obj;
extern s32 func_80033634_ovl_01(void);
extern void func_80166900_ovl_12(Obj*, s32, s32);
typedef void (*Fn)(void);
extern volatile Fn D_80166EC8_ovl_12[];
void func_80165BDC_ovl_12(Obj *a) {
  D_80166EC8_ovl_12[a->x[6]]();
  func_80166900_ovl_12(a, 3, 0);
  func_80166900_ovl_12(a, 2, 0);
  func_80166900_ovl_12(a, 2, (func_80033634_ovl_01() & 1) + 1);
}
Everything matches except: target emits `lui $v1,%hi(func_80166EA8_ovl_30+0x20)` as the 3rd insn, BEFORE `sw $ra,0x14($sp)` and before `lbu 6($s0)`; ours emits it after the lbu. This is exactly proposal #6's known KMC expander/sched1 limitation (target hoists %hi before sw ra, this build cannot). Tried: volatile element table (required; non-volatile folds to $at form and scores much worse), local volatile Fn *tbl, local Fn fn, pointer-to-element `&D[a->x[6]]` — all identical 60. search_flags: all -O2/-O3/-G8 variants 60, -fno-schedule-insns 120, -O1 330; flags are not the issue. No C shape found that moves the %hi up. Remaining fix needs the proposed split-address/scheduler KMC build.

Best stored 570 (autopilot m2c); my best manual attempt 580. My source (580) is a switch on a->x[6]:
case0: u16 t = *(volatile u16*)(a->x+0x2C8); *(volatile s32*)(a->x+0x1E8)=0x04000F00; *(volatile u8*)(a->x+0x2CE)=3; *(volatile u16*)(a->x+6)=0x100; *(volatile u16*)(a->x+0x1F4)=0; *(volatile u16*)(a->x+0x2C8)=t&0xFFFD; /* fallthrough */
case1: if (func_8008A974_ovl_01(a,b,c,0x100)!=0){ u8 s=*(volatile u8*)(a->x+6); *(volatile s32*)(a->x+0x1E8)=0x04000300; *(volatile u8*)(a->x+6)=s+1; } break;
case2: if (func_8008A974_ovl_01(a,b,c,0x400)!=0) *(volatile s32*)(a->x+4)=0x01040000; break;
then u8 cnt=*(volatile u8*)(a->x+7)+1; *(volatile u8*)(a->x+7)=cnt; if(cnt>=0xB){ p=func_80162820_ovl_14(a); *(volatile void**)(a->x+0x2C4)=p; if(p) *(volatile s32*)(a->x+4)=0x010F0000; }
Signature (Obj*, s32, s32): a1/a2 are passed straight through to func_8008A974 (same convention as matched func_80163480_ovl_16).

Only remaining diff for the 580 attempt: target emits plain `beqz $v0` for the state<2 test and fills delay slots with `lui $v0,0x400` / `lui $v1,0x400`; KMC emits `beql` and `nop`, which also yields one extra insn in case1. Everything else (operands, regalloc, body) matches. Tried no-effect: u8 st local + switch, s32 local constants, explicit default, store/load order in case1, volatile/non-volatile switch expr. Goto/if-chain form was worse (970, produced sltiu and bne). search_flags: project flags are optimal. The key is getting `addiu v0,zero,2` scheduled in the fall-through block so reorg must keep a plain branch; a switch alone puts it in the taken block and yields beql.

Best 7643 (if/else version; a real switch scored 8858 because this cc1 emits beql/nops, target uses beqz/decision tree). Worked-out structure:
- func_800275F8_ovl_01(3, 0x7C00, 0, 0); s0 = D_80160B9C_ovl_03; off = 0x18000.
- state = s0[2]:
  * 0: s0[0xA] += 2; func_8015F95C_ovl_03(0, s0[0xA], 0); if ((s8)s0[0xA] < 0) { s0[0xA]=0x80; s0[2]=s0[2]+1; }
  * 1: s0[0xB] += 1; func_8015F95C_ovl_03(s0[0xB], 0x80, 0); if ((s8)s0[0xB] < 0) { s0[0xB]=0x80; *(s16*)(s0+0x12)=-0x100; *(s16*)(s0+0x10)=0x5A00; s0[2]=s0[2]+1; }
  * 2: func_8015F95C_ovl_03(0x80,0x80,0); *(s16*)(s0+0x10) += *(s16*)(s0+0x12); func_800275F8_ovl_01(3, *(s16*)(s0+0x10), 0, 0); if (func_800276B4_ovl_01(3) != 0) { func_80077B0C_ovl_01(0x04060000,0); *(s16*)(s0+0x12)=0; *(s16*)(s0+0x10)=-1; s0[1]=1; }
  * default: nothing.
- b = D_800C7650_ovl_01 + off; if (*(u16*)(b+0x25E) & 0x9FF) { func_80077B0C_ovl_01(0x04060000,0); *(s16*)(D_80160B9C_ovl_03+0x12) = -0x1000; D_80160B9C_ovl_03[1]++; if (*(s16*)(D_80160B9C_ovl_03+0x10)==0) *(s16*)(D_80160B9C_ovl_03+0x10)=0x5A00; }
BLOCKER: the ROM materialises symbol addresses with split-address codegen: `lui v0,%hi(D_80160B9C); addiu s0,v0,%lo(D_80160B9C)` then uses 2(s0)/0xA(s0)/..., and `lui v0,1; ori v0,0x8000; lui v1,%hi(D_800C7650); addiu v1; addu v1,v1,v0; lhu 0x25E(v1)`. This cc1 folds every one of those into `%lo(D+offset)(reg)` and never keeps s0 (so the frame is 0x18 instead of 0x20 and s1 is not used). Same project-wide split-address limitation; needs the split-address KMC build.

MATCHED byte-identically. What made it match was the LOCAL DECLARATION ORDER (the guide's point, vindicated): declaring the locals in the same order as the values' first use - `s32 *obj; s32 *buf; u8 *p; s32 n;` - produced the ROM's exact s2/s3/s4/s1 assignment. My earlier version with the same logic but `s32 *s2; s32 s3; u8 *s1; s32 s0;` (and a different assignment order) scored 145 with s3/s4 swapped. Working C:
```c
typedef struct { u16 f; u8 pad[0x1C8 - 2]; } E1C8;   /* only needed by neighbours */
void func_8004CF60_ovl_01(void **arg0) {
    s32 *obj; s32 *buf; u8 *p; s32 n;
    obj = (s32 *)func_8008B028_ovl_01(0x10);
    obj[0] = 0x12C;
    buf = (s32 *)func_8008B0CC_ovl_01(0x4B0);
    p = (u8 *)buf;
    n = 0x12B;
    do {
        func_8004D1B4_ovl_01(p + 2);
        n -= 1;
        p += 4;
    } while (n != -1);
    obj[1] = (s32)buf;
    func_8004D1A8_ovl_01(obj);
    *arg0 = obj;
    return (s32)arg0;
}
```
Also: the loop must be `do {} while (n != -1)` with n starting 0x12B, and the store `obj[1] = buf` must come before the func_8004D1A8 call (the ROM's sw lands in the jal delay slot).

The claimed range is two functions (boundary reported: first ends at 0x80028B4C). The FIRST function (63 insns) is essentially matched — all calls, struct offsets, constants and control flow are right; only 4 tiny diffs remain:
- prologue saves s2 before ra (ROM) vs ra before s2 (mine);
- in the `unk_10 != 0xCB` block the `lw v0,4(v0)` is scheduled after `move a0,s0` in ROM;
- `beqz v1,.L80028B08` delay slot is `move a0,s0` in ROM vs `nop` in mine (the unk_08 != NULL branch);
- final `lw v1,0x78; lw v0,0x10(sp); addu v1,v1,v0` vs mine's v0/v1 swap.
The remaining 75 "missing" instructions are the second function, which starts at 0x80028B4C (different prologue: s0 at 0x10, uses s3, checks unk_10 against 0xCC, uses 0x108/0x10C/0x16C).

Working C for the first function (uses local typedefs):
struct S_sub28A50 { void (*unk_00)(void); void (*unk_04)(S_28A50*,s32); u8 unk_08[0xC]; s32 unk_14; s32 unk_18; };
